body:
> The contract of RefCounted is that the final owner is responsible for freeing it. As in this case Variant does not own this object for the arguement we made above, it can just view it as a normal object and do what it does to a normal object.

I agree, once refcount == 0 the reference effectively becomse a `weakref`..
The only way this object can be accessed at that point as a variant is through the `NOTIFICATION_PREDELETE` and `NOTIFICATION_PREDELETE_CLEANUP` notifications.

Documentation should be added to `RefCounted` and/or those symbols to clarify this specific case though! Otherwise people might store this weakref variant object and then use-after-free it later.. (but there's currently lots of ways you can accidentally do that with nodes and objects anyways)

Sanity check: the code change itself looks ok to me. Here's the relevant program flow as I see it:
```cpp
Variant::ObjData::unref():
...
		if (reference->unreference()) { //<< variant returns true when refcount == 0
			memdelete(reference); // calls predelete_handler() -> _predelete() and then ~T()
		}
		
```
`NOTIFICATION_PREDELETE` is sent before `~T()` but after refcount == 0
```cpp
bool Object::_predelete() {
...
	notification(NOTIFICATION_PREDELETE, true);
```

References:

https://github.com/godotengine/godot/blob/f40646901b1bb3e43322533c6981f1b2f6d5e387/core/variant/variant.cpp#L1136
```cpp
void Variant::ObjData::unref() {
	// Mirrors Ref::unref in refcounted.h
	if (id.is_ref_counted()) {
		RefCounted *reference = static_cast<RefCounted *>(obj);
		// Assuming reference is not null because id.is_ref_counted() was true.
		if (reference->unreference()) { //<< variant returns true when refcount == 0
			memdelete(reference); // calls predelete_handler() and then ~T()
		}
	}
	*this = ObjData();
}
```

https://github.com/godotengine/godot/blob/a65954894670d3c1f23d7e09c13df9cd0cf534a8/core/os/memory.h#L135
```cpp
template <typename T>
void memdelete(T *p_class) {
	if (!predelete_handler(p_class)) {
		return; // doesn't want to be deleted
	}
	if constexpr (!std::is_trivially_destructible_v<T>) {
		p_class->~T();
	}

	Memory::free_static(p_class, false);
}
```

https://github.com/godotengine/godot/blob/f40646901b1bb3e43322533c6981f1b2f6d5e387/core/object/object.cpp#L197
```cpp
bool Object::_predelete() {
	_predelete_ok = 1;
	notification(NOTIFICATION_PREDELETE, true);
	if (_predelete_ok) {
		_class_name_ptr = nullptr; // Must restore, so constructors/destructors have proper class name access at each stage.
		notification(NOTIFICATION_PREDELETE_CLEANUP, true);
	}
	return _predelete_ok;
}
```

I wish that the core team could take some time to review this PR. I today by chance found that this bug has been known for very long time, first reported back in 2016, and has been kept mentioning many times via comments and new issues. I have found three still opened issues about it:

https://github.com/godotengine/godot/issues/80834
https://github.com/godotengine/godot/issues/6784
https://github.com/godotengine/godot/issues/31166

and a proposal:
https://github.com/godotengine/godot-proposals/issues/11774

It is clear that the bug has caused quite some troubles. To make some help, let me briefly summarize the situation. When the reference count of a RefCounted object reaches zero, the object is deleted. This triggers the PREDELETE notification of the object.

```c++
// core/object/ref_counted.h#L202
if (reinterpret_cast<RefCounted *>(reference)->unreference()) {
  memdelete(reinterpret_cast<RefCounted *>(reference)); // calls predelete notification and deletes
}
```

In order to do something in the PREDELTE notification, we need the `self` object to reference the object itself. it is assigned to a `Variant`. However, `Variant` refuses to accept a count-zero object because the variant cannot do proper reference counting on it.

```c++
// core/variant/variant.cpp#L1094
if (id.is_ref_counted()) {
	RefCounted *reference = static_cast<RefCounted *>(obj);
	// In our case, reference() fails, so the object becomes empty
	if (!reference->reference()) {
		*this = ObjData();
	}
}
```

therefore, `self` remains empty and we cannot access any member of the `RefCounted` object, causing the issue. Note that `RefCounted` has no problem at all, `Variant` is the key of this issue.

The solution offered by this PR is simple. First, we need to identify this situation. We achieve this by checking if the reference count of the object is zero. Usually, we cannot see an object with zero reference count wandering around, because an object gets immediately deleted once the count reaches zero. PREDELETE notification seems to be the only exception. Next, we do not reference count such object, as we know it does not work. We simply treat it exactly the same as a regular object. When the variant storage changes, we do the testing again and skip the dereference counting if the count is already zero. See Files changed.

By doing so, normal reference counting is not affected at all, because the reference count of living objects can't be zero, as predeletion stage is the only known exception. And in this exceptional situation, the after-fix behavior of Variant to this RefCounted object has no difference from to a regular Object, and this is exactly what we expect. Note that this does not mean the object won't get freed, as we are still in the memdelete call of whoever makes the count zero: after the predeletion notification, the object will be freed immediately for certain.
